@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')Resources@endsection
@section('description')A resource connects a MariaDB or MySQL table to the panel. You write it by hand, or #[derive(IgniteResource)] writes it from an Orbit model.@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>A resource connects a database table to the panel. The panel lists, creates, edits and deletes its rows. A resource is a Rust type that implements the <code>IgniteResource</code> trait. You can write the impl by hand, or let <code>#[derive(IgniteResource)]</code> write it from an Orbit model. A resource can also be defined by data while the app runs: see <a href="#runtime-resources">Runtime resources</a>.</p>

  <h2>Address and name</h2>
  <p>Four constants name the resource:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>SLUG</code> is the URL segment. A slug of <code>articles</code> mounts the resource at <code>/admin/articles</code>. Two resources cannot share a slug.</li>
    <li><code>TITLE</code> is the heading of the list and the navigation label, such as <code>Articles</code>.</li>
    <li><code>SINGULAR</code> is the name of one record, used in the headings <code>New Tag</code> and <code>Edit Tag #3</code>. It defaults to <code>Record</code>, so a hand-written resource should set it.</li>
    <li><code>PER_PAGE</code> is the number of rows on a list page. It is 20 unless you change it, as in <code>const PER_PAGE: i64 = 50;</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The derive takes the slug from the table name, and the title from the table name with a capital letter, unless you set <code>title</code>. The singular name is the title without a trailing <code>s</code>, unless you set <code>singular</code>.</p>

  <h2>Derive a resource</h2>
  <p>The derive reads an Orbit model. The model also derives <code>OrbitModel</code> and <code>Serialize</code>, because the derive does not write them. The derive writes the form, the list, the queries and the input struct. This model has a <code>title</code> that is searchable and sortable, and a <code>body</code> that is a multi-line field and hidden from the list:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_core::IgniteResource;
use orbit_orm::OrbitModel;
use serde::Serialize;

#[derive(OrbitModel, Serialize, IgniteResource)]
#[orbit(table = "articles")]
#[ignite(
    table = articles,
    title = "Articles",
    latest_records(limit = 5, columns = "title,published_on"),
)]
pub struct Article {
    pub id: i64,
    #[orbit(fillable)]
    #[ignite(searchable, sortable)]
    pub title: String,
    #[orbit(fillable)]
    #[ignite(form_type = "textarea", hidden_on_index)]
    pub body: String,
    #[orbit(fillable)]
    pub published: bool,
    #[orbit(fillable)]
    #[ignite(label = "Published on")]
    pub published_on: Option&lt;chrono::NaiveDate&gt;,
}</code></pre>
  <p>The model's own attributes belong to Orbit. <code>#[orbit(table = "...")]</code> names the table, and when it is left out, Orbit derives the table name from the struct. <code>#[orbit(primary_key = "...")]</code> names the key column when it is not <code>id</code>. On a field, <code>#[orbit(column = "...")]</code> names a column that differs from the field name, <code>#[orbit(skip)]</code> keeps a field out of the table, so it gets its default value when a row is read, and <code>#[orbit(fillable)]</code> allows the column to be written.</p>
  <p>Every field of the form is written on a create and on an update, so mark each one <code>#[orbit(fillable)]</code>. See <a href="#saving">Saving and clearing a field</a> for what a blank field writes. Orbit refuses to write a column that is not fillable, and the save fails with its <code>NotFillable</code> error. The primary key is not in the form, so it needs no mark.</p>

  <p>The struct attributes are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>table</code>, the table of the model. Required. It must be the table of the Orbit model: the name in <code>#[orbit(table = ...)]</code>, or the name Orbit derives from the struct when that is left out. A different name is a compile error.</li>
    <li><code>title</code>, the heading of the list. Defaults to the table name, capitalized.</li>
    <li><code>singular</code>, the name of one record, for the headings. Defaults to the title without a trailing <code>s</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The field attributes are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>primary_key</code>, for a key that is not named <code>id</code>. The key must be an <code>i32</code> or an <code>i64</code>. It is left out of the form.</li>
    <li><code>label</code>, the label on the form and the column heading. Defaults to the field name, capitalized, with underscores as spaces.</li>
    <li><code>form_type</code>, one of <code>text</code>, <code>textarea</code>, <code>password</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>checkbox</code> or <code>date</code>. Defaults from the type: text for <code>String</code>, number for the integers, a decimal number for <code>f32</code> and <code>f64</code>, checkbox for <code>bool</code>, and date for <code>chrono::NaiveDate</code>. A date and time, <code>chrono::NaiveDateTime</code>, has its own input and needs no <code>form_type</code>. A <code>password</code> must be a <code>String</code>. See <a href="#passwords">Passwords</a>.</li>
    <li><code>options</code>, a select. A <code>String</code> field with a list of <code>("value", "Label")</code> pairs gives a fixed select. A <code>String</code> or integer field with a function, such as <code>options = load_categories</code>, loads its choices each time the form is shown. See the selects below.</li>
    <li><code>belongs_to</code> and <code>display</code>, a relation, which go together. <code>belongs_to = Category</code> names the related model, an <code>OrbitModel</code>, and <code>display</code> names the column of that model to show. See the selects below.</li>
    <li><code>step</code>, the browser's step for an <code>f32</code> or <code>f64</code> input, such as <code>step = "0.01"</code>. The default is <code>"any"</code>.</li>
    <li><code>required</code>, or <code>required = false</code>. A field that is not an <code>Option</code> is required by default, and a <code>bool</code> is never blank.</li>
    <li><code>searchable</code> and <code>sortable</code>, for the search box and the column headings. See <a href="/docs/tables">Tables</a>.</li>
    <li><code>hidden_on_index</code>, which leaves the column out of the list. The list shows every other field, including the <code>id</code>, unless you hide it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The supported field types are <code>String</code>, <code>i16</code>, <code>i32</code>, <code>i64</code>, <code>f32</code>, <code>f64</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>chrono::NaiveDate</code> and <code>chrono::NaiveDateTime</code>, and an <code>Option</code> of any of them. The primary key must be an <code>i32</code> or an <code>i64</code>. The derive refuses another type at compile time.</p>
  <p>The selects are written on the field, and the form shows them as a list:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">#[ignite(options = [("draft", "Draft"), ("published", "Published")])]
pub status: String,

#[ignite(options = load_categories)]
pub tag: Option&lt;String&gt;,

#[ignite(belongs_to = Category, display = name)]
pub category_id: Option&lt;i32&gt;,</code></pre>
  <p><code>load_categories</code> is an async function, <code>async fn load_categories(db: &amp;Database) -> Result&lt;Vec&lt;(String, String)&gt;, orbit_orm::Error&gt;</code>, and the panel calls it each time it shows the form. A <code>belongs_to</code> field stores the related row's key, so it is an <code>i32</code> or an <code>i64</code>. Its select lists the rows of the related model by their <code>display</code> column, and the list shows that name. A submitted value must be one of the choices, so a value with no row is refused. The derive does not write a foreign key: add one in the migration if the database should enforce it.</p>
  <p>The struct also takes <code>latest_records(...)</code>, which declares the dashboard table of the newest records. The <code>columns</code> are a comma-separated list of model fields to show, and are required. A password field is refused. <code>limit</code> is the most rows to show, and defaults to 5. <code>title</code> is the table's heading, and defaults to "Latest" and the resource title, so "Latest Articles". The derive writes <code>latest_records</code> from it, and <code>latest</code>, which orders by the primary key, newest first. See <a href="/docs/widgets">Widgets</a>.</p>
  <p>The derive also writes an input struct named after the model with <code>Input</code> added, here <code>ArticleInput</code>, which holds the validated form values. In Ignite 0.3.0 it also has a hidden <code>__ignite_sent</code> field, the optional fields the form sent, which <code>validate</code> and <code>validate_update</code> fill. Code that builds the input itself with a struct literal adds <code>__ignite_sent: Vec::new()</code>: on update, each optional field that is <code>None</code> then keeps its stored value. List a field's name, as in <code>vec!["published_on"]</code>, to write NULL for it. Without the line, the literal does not compile. See <a href="#saving">Saving and clearing a field</a>.</p>
  <p>The derive writes the trait impl, so it cannot write dashboard cards, which need a hand-written <code>widgets</code> method. See <a href="/docs/widgets">Widgets</a> and <a href="/docs/forms">Forms</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="passwords">Passwords</h2>
  <p>A field with <code>form_type = "password"</code> is hashed on save. This model keeps the login of an author:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_core::IgniteResource;
use orbit_orm::OrbitModel;
use serde::Serialize;

/// The password is hashed on save. It is required on create, and a blank
/// value on edit keeps the stored hash.
#[derive(OrbitModel, Serialize, IgniteResource)]
#[orbit(table = "authors")]
#[ignite(table = authors, title = "Authors", singular = "Author")]
pub struct Author {
    pub id: i64,
    #[orbit(fillable)]
    #[ignite(searchable, sortable)]
    pub name: String,
    #[orbit(fillable)]
    #[ignite(form_type = "password")]
    pub password: String,
}</code></pre>
  <p>The derive stores the argon2 hash from <code>ignite_core::hash_password</code>, the same function the panel's sign-in uses. The password is required on create. On edit, a blank password is optional, and it keeps the stored hash. Password fields are not list columns, and the edit form never shows the stored value. The derive refuses a password field that is not a <code>String</code> or an <code>Option</code> of one, at compile time.</p>

  <h2 id="saving">Saving and clearing a field</h2>
  <p><strong>Ignite 0.3.0: not yet at the pinned revision.</strong> This section describes Ignite 0.3.0, which is not published yet. The revision <code>d3d1975</code> that <a href="/docs/install">Installation</a> pins does not have it. At that revision, a blank optional field on edit keeps its old value.</p>
  <p>A derived resource writes the fields of the form on create and on update. A blank field that is an <code>Option</code> is saved as NULL, on edit as on create. So a user clears an optional value, such as the <code>published_on</code> date above, by emptying the field and saving the edit form.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>An <code>Option</code> field sent blank: NULL, on create and on edit.</li>
    <li>An <code>Option</code> field the request does not send at all: NULL on create; on edit it is not written, so the stored value is kept. The panel's own forms send every field, so this matters to an app that posts a partial form. <code>FormInput::has</code> tells a field sent blank from one not sent.</li>
    <li>A password left blank: required on create; on edit it is not written, so the stored hash is kept. This is the one exception.</li>
    <li>A field that is not an <code>Option</code>: required, on create and on edit. A blank one is refused with <code>&lt;label&gt; is required.</code> and nothing is saved.</li>
    <li>A checkbox: never blank. Unticked, or not sent, is <code>false</code>, because an unticked HTML checkbox sends nothing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Since Ignite 0.3.0. Before 0.3.0, a blank optional field was skipped on edit and kept its old value, so an optional field could not be cleared. An existing value now changes to NULL when a user saves the edit form with that field blank. A <a href="#runtime-resources">runtime resource</a> saves a blank optional field the same way.</p>

  <h2>Write a resource by hand</h2>
  <p>A hand-written resource implements the trait itself. The trait's query methods are async, and they take the app's Orbit <code>Database</code>. This one manages a <code>tags</code> table with a choice field, a search and a sort, a dashboard card and a dashboard table of its newest tags:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_core::{
    escape_like, Column, Field, FieldErrors, FieldKind, FormInput, IgniteResource, LatestRecords,
    ListQuery, Page, StatWidget,
};
use orbit_orm::{Database, Direction, Error, Model, OrbitModel, Operator, Value};
use serde::Serialize;

const KINDS: &amp;[(&amp;str, &amp;str)] = &amp;[("color", "Colour"), ("topic", "Topic")];

const NAME: Field = Field {
    name: "name",
    label: "Name",
    kind: FieldKind::Text,
    required: true,
};

const KIND: Field = Field {
    name: "kind",
    label: "Kind",
    kind: FieldKind::Select(KINDS),
    required: true,
};

#[derive(OrbitModel, Serialize)]
#[orbit(table = "tags")]
pub struct TagRecord {
    pub id: i64,
    #[orbit(fillable)]
    pub name: String,
    #[orbit(fillable)]
    pub kind: String,
}

pub struct TagInput {
    pub name: String,
    pub kind: String,
}

pub struct Tag;

impl IgniteResource for Tag {
    type Record = TagRecord;
    type Input = TagInput;

    const SLUG: &amp;'static str = "tags";
    const TITLE: &amp;'static str = "Tags";
    const SINGULAR: &amp;'static str = "Tag";

    async fn widgets(db: &amp;Database) -&gt; Result&lt;Vec&lt;StatWidget&gt;, Error&gt; {
        let total = TagRecord::query().count(db).await?;
        Ok(vec![StatWidget {
            label: "Tags",
            value: total,
        }])
    }

    fn latest_records() -&gt; Option&lt;LatestRecords&gt; {
        Some(LatestRecords {
            title: "Latest tags",
            columns: &amp;["name", "kind"],
            limit: 5,
        })
    }

    async fn latest(db: &amp;Database, limit: i64) -&gt; Result&lt;Vec&lt;TagRecord&gt;, Error&gt; {
        TagRecord::query()
            .order_by("id", Direction::Desc)
            .limit(limit.max(0) as u64)
            .get(db)
            .await
    }

    fn fields() -&gt; &amp;'static [Field] {
        &amp;[NAME, KIND]
    }

    fn columns() -&gt; &amp;'static [Column] {
        &amp;[
            Column {
                key: "name",
                label: "Name",
                sortable: true,
                searchable: true,
            },
            Column {
                key: "kind",
                label: "Kind",
                sortable: false,
                searchable: false,
            },
        ]
    }

    async fn list(db: &amp;Database, query: &amp;ListQuery) -&gt; Result&lt;Page&lt;TagRecord&gt;, Error&gt; {
        let pattern = query
            .search
            .as_ref()
            .map(|text| format!("%{}%", escape_like(text)));
        let mut rows = TagRecord::query();
        let mut count = TagRecord::query();
        if let Some(pattern) = &amp;pattern {
            rows = rows.where_op("name", Operator::Like, pattern.clone());
            count = count.where_op("name", Operator::Like, pattern.clone());
        }
        let (column, direction) = match &amp;query.sort {
            Some((key, ignite_core::Direction::Asc)) if key == "name" =&gt; ("name", Direction::Asc),
            Some((key, ignite_core::Direction::Desc)) if key == "name" =&gt; ("name", Direction::Desc),
            _ =&gt; ("id", Direction::Asc),
        };
        rows = rows.order_by(column, direction);

        let total = count.count(db).await?;
        let rows = rows
            .limit(query.per_page.max(0) as u64)
            .offset(query.offset().max(0) as u64)
            .get(db)
            .await?;
        Ok(Page { rows, total })
    }

    async fn find(db: &amp;Database, id: i64) -&gt; Result&lt;Option&lt;TagRecord&gt;, Error&gt; {
        TagRecord::query().find(db, id).await
    }

    async fn create(db: &amp;Database, input: TagInput) -&gt; Result&lt;TagRecord, Error&gt; {
        let values = [
            ("name", Value::from(input.name.as_str())),
            ("kind", Value::from(input.kind.as_str())),
        ];
        let id = TagRecord::query().insert(db, &amp;values).await?;
        Self::find(db, id as i64)
            .await?
            .ok_or(Error::NotFound { table: TagRecord::TABLE })
    }

    async fn update(db: &amp;Database, id: i64, input: TagInput) -&gt; Result&lt;TagRecord, Error&gt; {
        let values = [
            ("name", Value::from(input.name.as_str())),
            ("kind", Value::from(input.kind.as_str())),
        ];
        let changed = TagRecord::query()
            .where_eq("id", id)
            .update(db, &amp;values)
            .await?;
        if changed == 0 {
            return Err(Error::NotFound { table: TagRecord::TABLE });
        }
        Self::find(db, id)
            .await?
            .ok_or(Error::NotFound { table: TagRecord::TABLE })
    }

    async fn delete(db: &amp;Database, id: i64) -&gt; Result&lt;u64, Error&gt; {
        TagRecord::query().where_eq("id", id).delete(db).await
    }

    fn validate(form: &amp;FormInput) -&gt; Result&lt;TagInput, FieldErrors&gt; {
        let mut errors = FieldErrors::new();
        let name = NAME.check_required(form, &amp;mut errors);
        let kind = KIND.check_required(form, &amp;mut errors);
        let (Some(name), Some(kind)) = (name, kind) else {
            return Err(errors);
        };
        if name.chars().count() &gt; 50 {
            // A rule of the resource's own: the key is the field name, as a String.
            errors.insert("name".to_string(), "Name must be at most 50 characters.".to_string());
            return Err(errors);
        }
        if !KIND.check_choice(kind, KINDS, &amp;mut errors) {
            return Err(errors);
        }
        Ok(TagInput {
            name: name.to_string(),
            kind: kind.to_string(),
        })
    }
}</code></pre>
  <p>The methods are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>fields</code> and <code>columns</code>, the form fields and the list columns, in display order. A column's key matches a field name.</li>
    <li><code>list</code>, which returns one page of rows for a <code>ListQuery</code>, with the total count. It must apply the search and the sort the query asks for, and it should sort by the primary key when no sort is given.</li>
    <li><code>find</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code> and <code>delete</code>, which work on one row. <code>create</code> and <code>update</code> take the validated <code>Input</code> and return the row as the <code>Record</code>.</li>
    <li><code>validate</code>, which turns the submitted form into the <code>Input</code>, or returns an error message for each field. Call <code>check_required</code> for each required field, and <code>check_choice</code> for a select.</li>
    <li><code>validate_update</code>, which does the same for an edit. It defaults to <code>validate</code>. Override it when a field may be blank on edit.</li>
    <li><code>widgets</code>, the dashboard cards, and <code>latest_records</code> with <code>latest</code>, the dashboard table of the newest records. See <a href="/docs/widgets">Widgets</a>.</li>
    <li><code>options</code>, the choices of a <code>Lookup</code> field by its name, which the panel calls when it shows the form and when it checks a submitted value. The default has no choices. A derived resource writes it for <code>options = ...</code> and <code>belongs_to = ...</code>, and a hand-written resource with such a field implements it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A hand-written resource with a password must hash it with <code>ignite_core::hash_password</code> itself, and override <code>validate_update</code> so that a blank password keeps the stored hash on edit. A hand-written resource also decides what its <code>update</code> writes for a blank optional field. To match a derived resource, write NULL.</p>

  <h3 id="field-errors">Field errors</h3>
  <p><strong>Ignite 0.3.0: not yet at the pinned revision.</strong> This section describes Ignite 0.3.0, which is not published yet. The revision <code>d3d1975</code> that <a href="/docs/install">Installation</a> pins does not have it. At that revision, the keys of <code>FieldErrors</code> are <code>&amp;'static str</code>.</p>
  <p><code>validate</code> returns a <code>FieldErrors</code> when the form is refused. It is a <code>HashMap&lt;String, String&gt;</code>: the key is the field name and the value is the message shown under that field, as Laravel's <code>MessageBag</code> keys its messages by field. The checks on <code>Field</code>, such as <code>check_required</code>, <code>check_whole_number</code>, <code>check_decimal</code>, <code>check_date</code>, <code>check_datetime</code> and <code>check_choice</code>, record their message under the field's name. A rule of your own inserts the field name as a <code>String</code>, with <code>.to_string()</code> on a literal, as the name check in the example does. Reading a message takes a <code>&amp;str</code>: <code>errors.get("name")</code>.</p>
  <p>Before Ignite 0.3.0, the keys were <code>&amp;'static str</code>. A resource written for the main branch at <code>d3d1975</code> adds <code>.to_string()</code> to each literal key. A resource written for the 0.2.0 release has more to change, because Ignite moved from Diesel to Orbit after 0.2.0: <code>IgniteResource</code> is async and takes an Orbit <code>Database</code>, the model derives <code>OrbitModel</code> and marks its writable columns <code>#[orbit(fillable)]</code>, and the panel's new migrations run with <code>ignite migrate</code>. The 0.3.0 section of the Ignite changelog lists every change from 0.2.</p>
  <p>The <code>Record</code> is what the database returns. It must serialize to JSON with an <code>id</code> key holding the primary key as a number, and with keys named after the fields and columns. The panel builds the row links from <code>id</code>.</p>

  <h2>Add it to the panel</h2>
  <p>Pass each resource type to the panel builder, as <a href="/docs/install">Installation</a> shows: <code>.resource::&lt;Article&gt;()</code>, <code>.resource::&lt;Tag&gt;()</code> and <code>.resource::&lt;Author&gt;()</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="runtime-resources">Runtime resources</h2>
  <p><strong>Ignite 0.3.0: not yet at the pinned revision.</strong> This section describes Ignite 0.3.0, which is not published yet. The revision <code>d3d1975</code> that <a href="/docs/install">Installation</a> pins does not have it. Runtime resources are new in 0.3.0.</p>
  <p>A resource can also be defined by data while the app runs, such as a custom post type that a site owner creates in the admin. A runtime resource is a <code>RuntimeResource</code> value, not a Rust type. Its slug, titles, fields and columns are owned values, so the app can load them from the database, and its rows come from a <code>RuntimeData</code> that the app implements. The panel serves it at <code>/admin/&lt;slug&gt;</code> with the same list, forms, checks and delete action as a static resource, and shows it in the navigation, from the next request on.</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_core::{
    register_runtime, unregister_runtime, ColumnDef, FieldDef, FieldKindDef, RuntimeResource,
};

register_runtime(
    RuntimeResource::new("books", "Books", "Book", BookData)
        .field(FieldDef {
            name: "title".into(),
            label: "Title".into(),
            kind: FieldKindDef::Text,
            required: true,
        })
        .field(FieldDef {
            name: "pages".into(),
            label: "Pages".into(),
            kind: FieldKindDef::Number,
            required: false,
        })
        .column(ColumnDef {
            key: "title".into(),
            label: "Title".into(),
            sortable: true,
            searchable: true,
        })
        .per_page(50),
)?;
ignite_panel::register_runtime_permissions(&amp;db, "books").await?;

// Later, when the site owner deletes the post type:
unregister_runtime("books")?;</code></pre>

  <h3>The definition</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>RuntimeResource::new(slug, title, singular, data)</code> starts a resource with no fields or columns, 20 rows per page. <code>.field(...)</code> and <code>.column(...)</code> add one after the others, and <code>.per_page(n)</code> sets the rows per page.</li>
    <li><code>FieldDef</code> (<code>name</code>, <code>label</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>required</code>) is the owned twin of <code>Field</code>, and <code>FieldKindDef</code> of <code>FieldKind</code>: <code>Text</code>, <code>Textarea</code>, <code>Password</code>, <code>Number</code>, <code>Decimal(step)</code>, <code>Checkbox</code>, <code>Select(choices)</code>, <code>Lookup</code>, <code>Date</code> and <code>DateTime</code>. <code>FieldDef::from(field)</code> converts a static <code>Field</code>.</li>
    <li><code>ColumnDef</code> (<code>key</code>, <code>label</code>, <code>sortable</code>, <code>searchable</code>) is the owned twin of <code>Column</code>. <code>ColumnDef::from(column)</code> converts a static one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A field name must be a lowercase identifier: lowercase ASCII letters, digits and <code>_</code>, starting with a letter, and at most 64 bytes (<code>MAX_FIELD_NAME</code>), as a column name is. The slug is lowercase letters, digits, <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>, as one URL segment. Definitions are owned, so registering a resource again after an edit keeps nothing of the old one, and unregistering it frees all of it.</p>

  <h3>The data</h3>
  <p><code>RuntimeData</code> has the queries of <code>IgniteResource</code>, with rows as JSON maps (<code>Row</code>, a <code>serde_json::Map</code>): <code>list</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, and <code>options</code> for a <code>Lookup</code> field, which defaults to no choices. Each method returns a <code>RuntimeFuture</code>, so write the body as <code>Box::pin(async move { ... })</code>. Ignite assumes no table layout: a custom post type can keep its rows in a shared <code>posts</code> table and its values in a meta table. A row needs a numeric <code>id</code>, and a key for each field and column it shows.</p>
  <p>The panel validates the form before it calls <code>create</code> or <code>update</code>, with the same checks and messages as a static resource, and passes one key per field: text as a string, a number as an integer, a decimal as a number, a checkbox as <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>, a date as <code>"YYYY-MM-DD"</code>, a date and time as <code>"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"</code>, and a password as its hash. An optional field sent blank is <code>null</code>, on create and on edit, so <code>update</code> should store NULL and clear the value, as a derived resource does (see <a href="#saving">Saving and clearing a field</a>). On edit, an optional field the form did not send, and a password left blank, are left out of the row, so <code>update</code> keeps their stored values. A checkbox that is not sent is <code>false</code>. Form errors are keyed by the field's name in <code>FieldErrors</code>.</p>
  <p>A unique-index violation shows as <code>&lt;label&gt; is already taken.</code> only when the index is named <code>{slug}_{field}_unique</code>, as for a static resource. A <code>RuntimeData</code> that stores values elsewhere should check uniqueness itself.</p>

  <h3>Registering and removing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>register_runtime(resource)</code> adds the resource. It returns a <code>RuntimeError</code> when the definition or the slug is refused.</li>
    <li><code>unregister_runtime(slug)</code> removes it. From the next request on, it is gone from the navigation and its routes answer 404. Its permission rows stay, so the grants come back if it is registered again.</li>
    <li><code>runtime_resource(slug)</code> and <code>runtime_resources()</code> read the registry.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The variants of <code>RuntimeError</code> are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>InvalidSlug(slug)</code>: the slug is not one URL segment of lowercase letters, digits, <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>.</li>
    <li><code>SlugTaken(slug)</code>: a static resource, another runtime resource, or one of the panel's own pages (such as <code>login</code>) has the slug.</li>
    <li><code>InvalidFieldName { slug, field }</code>: a field name is not a lowercase identifier of at most 64 bytes.</li>
    <li><code>DuplicateField { slug, field }</code>: two fields have the same name.</li>
    <li><code>NotRegistered(slug)</code>: <code>unregister_runtime</code> found no runtime resource at the slug.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Permissions</h3>
  <p>A runtime resource has the same five abilities as a static one: <code>&lt;slug&gt;.viewAny</code>, <code>.view</code>, <code>.create</code>, <code>.update</code> and <code>.delete</code>. Call <code>ignite_panel::register_runtime_permissions(&amp;db, slug).await</code> right after <code>register_runtime</code>, in the same request, so the permission rows exist at once and a role can be granted them. Running it again changes nothing. It refuses a slug that is not registered, and creates nothing.</p>
  <p>If the app does not call it, the panel creates the rows itself on the next signed-in request, before it reads the user's abilities. The rows stay when the resource is removed. See <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a> for roles and permissions.</p>
  <p>A runtime resource has no dashboard cards or dashboard table yet.</p>
@endsection
